            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Lead with the Conclusion: Most Bans Are Preventable</h2>
<p>Overseas ad platforms are tightening their review processes, but most account bans aren't sudden attacks—they're the result of accumulated risk over time. During our service of hundreds of overseas accounts, Yiyu Shumei found that <strong>over 80% of account risk issues stem from missing inspection routines</strong>—either no inspection habit was established, or the inspection dimensions were incomplete.</p>
<p>This article skips the theory and goes straight to practical compliance inspection SOPs.</p>
<h2>High-Risk Point 1: Payment and Top-Up Anomalies</h2>
<p>This is the most likely to trigger risk controls but the easiest to overlook.</p>
<p><strong>Common issues include:</strong><br />
- Sudden large fluctuations in top-up amounts over short periods<br />
- Frequent changes to payment methods or cards<br />
- Accounts with long-term zero balance or sudden large top-ups after prolonged low spending<br />
- Mixing prepaid and postpaid accounts</p>
<p>One tool app team reported that after a large top-up at the beginning of the month, their account triggered platform review immediately. Investigation revealed that the operations staff had bound 3 credit cards from different regions simultaneously, triggering the platform's fraud detection mechanism.</p>
<p><strong>Inspection actions:</strong><br />
- Before topping up, confirm whether the account's historical spending curve is stable<br />
- Prefer stable payment methods, avoid frequent changes<br />
- Match top-up amounts with historical spending capacity</p>
<h2>High-Risk Point 2: Creative Compliance Gaps</h2>
<p>Creatives are a key focus of platform review and the area most prone to "pitfalls."</p>
<p><strong>Three most problematic directions:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Exaggerated or misleading statements</strong><br />
   Using absolute language like "100% effective" or "world's first," or using exaggerated promises on landing pages that don't match the creatives.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Copyright and authorization issues</strong><br />
   Using third-party brand logos, celebrity images, or copyrighted music/video clips without authorization. One cross-border e-commerce team received a platform takedown notice and had violation points deducted because a chorus from a popular song was used in their creative.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Inducement interaction tactics</strong><br />
   Inducement copy like "click to get" or "share to win cash" is high-risk content on multiple platforms.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Inspection actions:</strong><br />
- Check creatives against platform advertising policies before launching<br />
- Establish internal creative review checklists, including copyright declaration verification<br />
- Regularly review already-launched creatives and monitor platform policy updates</p>
<h2>High-Risk Point 3: Chaotic Account Permission Management</h2>
<p>Accounts managed by multiple people are most prone to permission leaks and operation chaos.</p>
<p><strong>Typical scenarios:</strong><br />
- Employees not having account access removed promptly after departure<br />
- Multiple people using the same account, causing frequent IP and device changes<br />
- Unclear permission configuration between Business Manager (BM) and ad accounts</p>
<p>The platform's risk control system records login behavior patterns. When an account logs in from three different countries within 24 hours, the system typically triggers security verification, and in serious cases, directly restricts advertising.</p>
<p><strong>Inspection actions:</strong><br />
- Establish standardized processes for employee permission changes, complete permission removal on the departure day<br />
- Restrict login permissions for high-risk regions<br />
- Use official partner tools to uniformly manage multi-account permissions</p>
<h2>Compliance Inspection Checklist (Ready to Copy)</h2>
<p><strong>Daily checks:</strong><br />
- Is the account's delivery status normal?<br />
- Is today's spending abnormal (sudden increase or decrease)?<br />
- Are there any platform notifications or violation warnings?</p>
<p><strong>Weekly checks:</strong><br />
- Is the payment method stable without new cards or abnormal changes?<br />
- Do creatives have compliance risks due to platform policy updates?<br />
- Are there abnormal IPs or devices in login records?</p>
<p><strong>Monthly checks:</strong><br />
- Permission list cleanup, confirm active employee permissions match their positions<br />
- Historical delivery data review, investigate suspected abnormal spending<br />
- Has the account health score or credit score declined?</p>
<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>Compliance isn't a one-time task—it's an ongoing habit. Only by systematizing inspection processes can you maintain account stability in an environment where platform rules are constantly tightening.</p>
